    <section class="band">
        <div class="section-shell">
            <h2 class="section-title">Questions International Patients Ask</h2>
            <p class="section-lead">Real questions we answer every day — with honest, no-fluff answers.</p>
            <div class="faq-grid">
                <div class="faq-card">
                    <h3>🇨🇳 Is it safe to have surgery in China?</h3>
                    <p>At the hospitals we recommend, safety is anchored to <strong>JCI accreditation</strong> and the national <strong>Grade 3A</strong> classification — China's highest hospital tier. Surgeons at hospitals like Fuwai perform <strong>15,000+ cardiac surgeries a year</strong>. That said, we only recommend hospitals we've verified ourselves — and we don't send patients anywhere we wouldn't send our own family.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-card">
                    <h3>🗣️ Do Chinese hospital staff speak English?</h3>
                    <p>The hospitals we recommend have <strong>dedicated international departments</strong> with English-speaking coordinators, translators, and doctors. Many top surgeons trained in the US, UK, or Germany and speak fluent English. Outside the international department, English is limited — which is why we match you to hospitals that are set up for foreign patients.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-card">
                    <h3>💰 How much does surgery cost, and how do I pay?</h3>
                    <p>Major surgery in China typically costs <strong>50–80% less</strong> than US private care: knee replacement <strong>$5,500–$9,000</strong>, heart bypass <strong>$10,000–$16,000</strong>, CAR-T therapy from <strong>$30,000</strong>. Hospitals accept <strong>cash, WeChat/Alipay, and major credit cards</strong> (Visa, Mastercard, Amex). Some accept international insurance with direct billing. We give you a clear cost estimate before you travel — no surprise bills.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-card">
                    <h3>🛂 How do I get a medical visa for China?</h3>
                    <p>Most patients apply for an <strong>S2 (short-term private visit) visa</strong> or an <strong>L (tourist) visa</strong>. Once you have a hospital appointment confirmation, the hospital can provide an <strong>invitation letter</strong> to support your application. For many nationalities, <strong>eVisa or Visa-on-Arrival</strong> options are available — and we guide you through which one applies to your passport.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-card">
                    <h3>🏠 What happens after surgery — follow-up care?</h3>
                    <p>Before you leave China, the hospital provides a <strong>discharge summary in English</strong> and a follow-up plan your doctor back home can follow. Many hospitals offer <strong>telemedicine follow-ups</strong> post-discharge. For complex recovery, we can coordinate a stay at a nearby serviced apartment before you fly home.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p style="text-align:center;margin-top:12px;color:#888;font-size:.92rem">Have a different question? <a href="/contact-new.html" style="color:#1e3c72;font-weight:700">Ask us directly →</a></p>
        </div>
    </section>

